# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ford_Anglia
# Ford Anglia 105E: the iconic "reverse-rake rear window" Anglia, launched in
# September 1959 and built to November 1967. 1,004,737 saloons and 205,001 vans
# were built; 191,752 came out of Dagenham in 1960 alone, then a production
# record for Ford of Britain. In 1965 it was the fourth best-selling car in
# Britain with 84,589 registrations (7.7% of a 1,098,887 market), behind the BMC
# 1100 157,679, the Cortina 116,985 and the Mini 104,477; 1966: 68,209 (fourth);
# 1967: 55,735 (fifth) (SMMT tables, AROnline and Auto Express). The 4-door twin
# by body (Ford Prefect 107E, 1959-61) is in the tree as FordPrefectLate.
#
# Dimensions: the Ford Anglia 105E Saloon Instruction Book general specification
# gives wheelbase 7 ft 6 1/2 in (2,299 mm), overall length 12 ft 9 1/2 in
# (3,900 mm), overall width 4 ft 9 1/8 in (1,451 mm) and overall height unladen
# 4 ft 8 5/8 in (1,436 mm); kerb weight 741 kg (997 cc). The Motor's 1959 road
# test gives 154 x 56 x 56 in (3,912 x 1,422 x 1,422 mm), which is what the
# Wikipedia infobox carries; the maker's figures are used here.
#
# Colours: Ambassador Blue, Savoy Black, Imperial Maroon, Ermine White and
# Spruce Green are real Ford of Britain paint names with their chassis-plate
# paint codes, taken from the Ford Anglia 105E paint-code list for 1959-1967
# (anglia-models.co.uk, "Paint Codes"): Savoy Black (A) 1959-Nov 1967, standard
# and deluxe; Ambassador Blue (M) 1959-1966, all models; Imperial Maroon (AZ)
# 1959-1966, deluxe/super/estate; Ermine White (BA) September 1960-November
# 1967, all models; Spruce Green (BZ) May 1963-November 1967, all models.
# Glacier Blue, Monaco Red, Goodwood Green, Lagoon Blue, Purbeck Grey and the
# other fifteen catalogue colours are omitted as the less common of the range.
#
# Colour shares: the DVSA first-registration table starts with 1994
# registrations, so its measured base for cars first registered in 1994-96 (blue
# 23-25%, red 19-23%, white 7-10%, black 8-10%, grey 4-5%, silver 9-11%, other
# 20-25%) is the starting point, adjusted for 1960s registrations: silver
# (9-11% measured) is reduced to the negligible pre-metallic share and omitted;
# black is raised (16%) because black was a mainstream 1960s colour and became a
# minority shade by the 1990s, from which the measured base is drawn; grey is not
# modelled although the 105E range had four greys; green (11%) is taken out of
# the measured "other" group; white is raised slightly to 13% (cream/white was
# commoner on 1960s registrations than on 1990s survivors); maroon is modelled
# at 15% in place of the measured red 19-23%, as Imperial Maroon is a darker
# shade than the measured red group. Shares used: blue 25%, black 16%, maroon
# 15%, white 13%, green 11%.
#
# DistributionWeight totals 370 across the five colourways, provisionally: the
# model is a strong non-anchor tiered below the 1955/1960 anchor (Morris Minor,
# 500 on the 2026-11-15 rebase) at roughly three-quarters of it. The 105E's parc
# at its mid-life was larger than the 100E's and about 70-75% of the Minor's
# (1,004,737 saloons built 1959-67 against about 885,000 Minors by end-1960,
# both with substantial export shares; the 1965 registration standing above
# confirms it as the era's leading small Ford). Provisional pending the central
# rebase. The 105E and 100E overlap briefly in road presence to the mid-1960s,
# so their combined weight exceeds the anchor's 500 in that window; this is a
# property of the per-generation convention and is for the central rebase.
#
# Speed 120 km/h: a game-balance figure, not the model's top speed. The Motor
# recorded 73.8 mph (119 km/h) for a 105E saloon in 1959. Comfort 50/255 is one
# step above the 100E's 45/250: the 105E is a newer body (1959) on the same
# MacPherson-strut front suspension, with better seating and a four-speed
# gearbox, which is a modest improvement on its era peers (FordPrefectLate
# 45/250, Morris Minor 45/250).
#
# Dates describe when each colour would be seen regularly on UK roads, not
# production dates. Production ended in November 1967; the cars rusted severely
# but a large parc lasted into the early 1970s, so retire dates run to 1973-76.
# Savoy Black, Ambassador Blue and Imperial Maroon enter with the September 1959
# launch; Ermine White follows the documented September 1960 start of that
# colour, Spruce Green the May 1963 start. The modelled front end is the Deluxe
# form with chrome bumpers and grille. The photo-referenced body includes the
# reverse-rake rear window, separate boot deck, raised rear wings and tall lamps.
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/ford-anglia-105e-photo.blend in the blends repository.
# Geometry and final colour renders approved on 2026-11-15.

